# AGENTS.md — {{PROJECT_NAME}} (Codex entry point)

## Source of truth

Read [CLAUDE.md](CLAUDE.md) and [CLAUDE.kit.md](CLAUDE.kit.md) in full before working here. They are the source of truth for both agents,
and every rule in them applies to Codex as written; read "Claude" as "the agent". Values written `<LIKE_THIS>` come
from the Project facts block in `CLAUDE.md`. This file adds only how Codex carries the rules out; it waives no gate.

## Non-negotiables (restated)

Codex loads this file, not `CLAUDE.md`, so the must-never-miss lines are copied here verbatim: the tagged lines of
`CLAUDE.kit.md`, then of `CLAUDE.md`, in order. CI fails on any difference. Edit the source, then copy.

<!-- codex-restate:start -->
- **No destructive filesystem or git commands** (deleting files, folders or branches, hard resets, cleans, discarding changes), whether run or written into code, docs or runbooks. Rename to `<file>.pre-agent-kit`, `git stash` (never drop), use a disposable worktree, or name the files for the owner to remove. Code may remove only temp dirs it created. <!-- codex-restate -->
- **No secrets** in code, logs, commits, PRs, ticket comments or docs: name the env var, never the value. Never open a secret file or print a token. Gitignore a new secret-bearing file in the same change. <!-- codex-restate -->
- **Never commit, push or force-push to `<MAIN_BRANCH>` directly**, and never force-push a shared branch. Work on a feature branch; `/ship` merges through a PR. <!-- codex-restate -->
- **No hand-run schema changes.** Only via the migration path in installed pack rules; never raw SQL from a console, tool or script. Schema stays additive; a removal is phased, and its final step is G5. <!-- codex-restate -->
- **No breaking API change.** Never remove or change existing fields, URLs or status codes; ship a new route or keep the old shape working (`.claude/rules/api-contracts.md`). <!-- codex-restate -->
- **No new dependency** unless `<DEPENDENCY_POLICY>` allows it; record why on the ticket or PR. <!-- codex-restate -->
- **No CI, deploy or release triggers** outside the owner-invoked commands that own them, which still stop at their gates. <!-- codex-restate -->
- **Never invent a ticket key, ID or URL.** Write only one you fetched or created this session. <!-- codex-restate -->
Agents decide everything else. These need the owner's explicit yes at the time: <!-- codex-restate -->
- **G1** Production builds and releases <!-- codex-restate -->
- **G2** Writes to production data, except rows the agent created with `<SYNTHETIC_MARKER>` <!-- codex-restate -->
- **G3** Merging to `<MAIN_BRANCH>`. With the ticket-trains pack installed, invoking `/ticket-train-run` approves that train's queue only <!-- codex-restate -->
- **G4** Admin-override merges, or bypassing branch protection <!-- codex-restate -->
- **G5** Destructive schema steps: DROP, final column removal, data deletion <!-- codex-restate -->
- **G6** Making data readable by clients: policies, grants, public buckets <!-- codex-restate -->
- **G7** Weakening or removing a safety check: CI checks, hooks, settings hook entries, these gates. Adding or tightening a check doesn't need the owner <!-- codex-restate -->
- **Project gates:** `<OWNER_KEPT_GATES>` <!-- codex-restate -->
A change to `<CRITICAL_SYSTEMS>` is agent-approved with stricter checks unless it hits a gate: (1) find every consumer; (2) record its need and risk on the ticket; (3) add tests, regressions included, keeping existing ones green; (4) verify before merge. A post-merge failure is fixed forward on a new ticket and alerts the owner. <!-- codex-restate -->
- **`/post-op` is never optional before a commit that touches source or `.claude/`.** Small, urgent, low-risk or well-tested is never a reason to skip it. <!-- codex-restate -->
<!-- codex-restate:end -->

The G lines are the Owner-Kept Gates; the "A change to one of" line is Critical Systems.

## Rule loading

`.claude/rules/*.md` are mandatory. Read each, where it is, before planning or editing in its area. If a required
rule can't be read, stop and ask.

| Rule | Read before |
|---|---|
| `.claude/rules/security.md` | Any task |
| `.claude/rules/code-style.md` | Any code, logging or new file |
| `.claude/rules/testing.md` | Any implementation, bug fix, test change or verification plan |
| `.claude/rules/api-contracts.md` | Any route, response shape, status code, field, client call or dependency |
| `.claude/rules/<pack rule>.md` | Its `paths:` frontmatter or header says when; installed packs add rows here |

Never copy them into `.agents/rules/` or `.codex/rules/` (which configures command approvals).

## Workflows

Each `.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` links to `.claude/commands/<name>.md`; read and follow that file. A Claude
command `/x` is the Codex skill `$x`. When a workflow says to run another `/command`, follow that command's file.
Each skill is a separate invocation, not the whole pipeline; keep the ticket keys and arguments given.

| Step | Claude | Codex |
|---|---|---|
| Plan | `/triage <ticket>` | `$triage <ticket>` |
| Build | `/operate` | `$operate` |
| Review own work | `/post-op` | `$post-op` |
| Post-op review round | consensus review, fallback `/code-review high` | `⏭️ Skipped — Codex` |
| Peer review (optional) | `/peer-review` | `$peer-review` |
| Ship | `/ship` | `$ship` |
| Supporting | `/verify`, `/test`, `/investigate`, `/handoff`, `/resume`, the gate commands | same name with `$` |

- Record the review-round row as `⏭️ Skipped — Codex`; it counts as passing for `$ship`. `⚠️ Not run` and
  `⚠️ N open` do not. Never report a step as run when it wasn't.
- `$peer-review` runs `$bteam` and `$codex-review`. It is optional; once a pass has run, an unresolved verified
  finding stops `$ship`.

## Codex as reviewer

- In ordinary work Codex may implement, test and document what the owner asks, and ship when authorized.
- As an independent reviewer (`codex-review`, `bteam`, `peer-review`), Codex is review-only: no edits. Return
  findings with `file:line` evidence and stated uncertainty. Reviewers never repair what they review.
- A Codex subagent is not a Claude review, and the implementer's own analysis is never an independent review. Never
  relabel one engine as another. An unavailable engine is recorded with its actual error, as partial coverage.

## Tooling adaptations

- **Tools:** use the Codex equivalent of a named tool. Check a connected service points at the right account before
  using it. Report a missing capability; never invent a result.
- **Claude-only features:** the Workflow tool, `AskUserQuestion` and subagent types don't exist in Codex. Ask the
  owner in chat, one question at a time, each option with a pro and a con. Claude hooks don't run under Codex, so
  apply what they guard by hand: never commit or push to `<MAIN_BRANCH>`, never run a destructive command.
- **Task lists:** turn `TaskCreate` / `TaskList` steps into a session checklist, and carry every open item into
  handoffs.
- **Session controls:** `/clear` and `/compact` are context steps, not shell commands. Tell the owner when a fresh
  session is needed; never claim context was cleared.
- **Permissions and state:** plain, permitted edits for handoffs and `.claude/state/`. Never hide a path or switch
  tools to evade a restriction. If memory is missing, say so; never claim to have read it.
- **Authorization:** loading a skill authorizes none of its side effects. A request to implement or run `$post-op`
  is not a request to ship. `$ship` authorizes its stated release; `$ticket-train-run` (ticket-trains pack) authorizes only that queue.

## Parity

If a shared file seems to conflict with this one, the shared file wins for the rule; this file wins only for how
Codex carries it out. Raise a real conflict as a bug before going on.

## Reporting

Separate checks that ran, failed, were unavailable or were waived. Take test counts from the runner. Never call uncommitted work committed, an unmerged branch shipped, or a static check runtime
verification. End with `Owner action: none` or the single action the owner must take now.
